---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'connect', label: 'Connect the server' },
   { id: 'run', label: 'The automated checks' },
   { id: 'driver', label: 'Screen reader sessions' },
   { id: 'prompts', label: 'Example prompts' },
   { id: 'pitfalls', label: 'Common mistakes' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Use with an AI agent"
   summary="The MCP server is a local process that your editor or coding agent starts. It exposes the a11ied commands as MCP tools, so the agent can run the automated checks, drive a screen reader, and look up criteria from the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) for the page you name. It runs over stdio and opens no network port."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="connect">
      <h2>Connect the server</h2>
      <p>Add a11ied to the MCP configuration your editor or agent reads, then restart it.</p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Point <code>command</code> at <code>a1</code> with <code>args: ["mcp"]</code> instead if
         you installed the package globally and want the client to use that copy.
      </p>
      <p>
         Ask the agent to call <code>doctor</code> first. The result says which browser a11ied
         uses and whether VoiceOver, NVDA, and the simulated screen reader are ready, so the
         agent knows which tools it can run on this machine.
      </p>
      <p>
         The server also serves seven read-only resources, including the criteria index and the
         test method of every criterion in WCAG 2.1 and 2.2. See the
         <a href="/reference/mcp">MCP reference</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="run">
      <h2>The automated checks</h2>
      <p>
         The automated checks run axe-core, the open-source rule engine most automated
         accessibility checkers use. a1 injects it into the page and runs it there, so it sees
         the rendered DOM, not just the HTML a server sent. Start your development server, then
         give the agent the exact route and the WCAG level you want tested.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Open http://localhost:3000/account/settings.
Use a11ied to run axe at WCAG level AA.
For each violation, give me the selector, the impact, and the failing HTML.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>run_axe</code> with <code>target</code> set to the URL and
         <code>level: "AA"</code>. Omitting <code>criterion</code>, <code>level</code>, and
         <code>ruleIds</code> runs every rule mapped to a WCAG criterion. The response has a
         <code>verdict</code> object and <code>exitCode</code> 4 when a violation was found.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="driver">
      <h2>Screen reader sessions</h2>
      <p>
         Use a session for labels, focus order, dialogs, menus, and validation messages. The
         automated checks do not test any of those in sequence.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Use a11ied to walk the checkout form with the screen reader.
Move through every field in order and collect every announcement.
Tell me which controls announce without a name and where the order
does not match the visual order.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>sr_session</code> with <code>action: "start"</code>, then
         <code>sr_action</code> for each step, then <code>sr_expect</code> to check that an
         announcement was made. For VoiceOver or NVDA, open the page in a browser before the agent
         starts. For the simulated screen reader, the agent passes <code>url</code> and
         <code>allowVirtual: true</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Remind the agent to call <code>sr_session</code> with <code>action: "stop"</code> when
         it finishes. A session left open keeps VoiceOver or NVDA under automation until
         something stops it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="prompts">
      <h2>Example prompts</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Review a branch</strong>
            <span>Run <code>run_axe</code> on the routes the diff touches and compare each violation against the intended behavior.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Test a dialog</strong>
            <span>Open it, move focus through it, close it, and report which element has focus at each step.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Work one criterion</strong>
            <span>Run <code>wcag_show</code> for the criterion, read its test method, run <code>audit</code> on the page, then run the test the <code>strategy</code> lists.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Narrow a bug report</strong>
            <span>Reproduce the reported step, run <code>sr_transcript</code>, and quote the exact announcement.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="pitfalls">
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <p>
         Reporting a passing axe run as meeting WCAG level AA. axe rules decide 8 of the 24
         level AA criteria. Ask the agent to run <code>wcag_criteria</code> with
         <code>summary: true</code> and report the <a href="/test-methods">test method</a> next
         to every result.
      </p>
      <p>
         Quoting the simulated screen reader as VoiceOver. The <code>sr_session</code> start
         response has <code>targetType</code>: <code>real</code> for VoiceOver and NVDA,
         <code>simulated</code> for the simulated screen reader. Ask the agent to include it in
         its report. The <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skill</a> puts both rules in the
         agent's instructions.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
